  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Washington received $13.2B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $8.8B, while the Department of Energy obligated $3.1B. Award activity totaled 82,571 actions, with an average award size of $160K. Obligations are down 16.3% year over year. Annual trends show variability: FY21 obligations were $15.6B, FY22 obligations were $12.1B, FY23 obligations were $13B, FY24 obligations were $15.4B, and FY25 obligations were $15.8B. THE BOEING COMPANY received $5.9B, making Aircraft Manufacturing the largest industry at $5.9B. BATTELLE MEMORIAL INSTITUTE received $1.2B, and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences totaled $1.2B. Hanford-related contracts and cleanup contractors appear among the largest vendors and industries: HANFORD TANK WASTE OPERATIONS & CLOSURE, LLC received $691M, BECHTEL NATIONAL, INC. received $627M, and Hazardous Waste Treatment and Disposal totaled $686M. This profile reflects large defense and energy program dollars concentrated in a small set of vendors and NAICS categories.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal spending in Washington?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $8.8B in obligations, followed by the Department of Energy with $3.1B. THE BOEING COMPANY received $5.9B, and Aircraft Manufacturing accounted for $5.9B, showing heavy concentration in a few vendors and industries. Total obligations of $13.2B came from 82,571 awards, so dollars are concentrated even though the award count is large."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors dominate federal work in Washington?",
        "body": "Aircraft Manufacturing leads with $5.9B, and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences totals $1.2B. Major vendors include THE BOEING COMPANY at $5.9B and BATTELLE MEMORIAL INSTITUTE at $1.2B, while several cleanup and construction contractors show multi-hundred-million-dollar obligations. Hazardous Waste Treatment and Disposal at $686M and Remediation Services at $589M highlight large environmental and cleanup programs."
      }
    ]
